    "result_text": "All 287 tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented a per-city owner limit that rejects owner creation with **HTTP 400** when the owner's city already contains 8 owners, following the existing exception-handling conventions in the codebase.\n\n**Production changes:**\n- **`CityOwnerLimitException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` mirroring the existing `DailyOwnerRegistrationLimitException`.\n- **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` mapping `CityOwnerLimitException` to a 400 `ProblemDetail`, matching the daily-limit handler.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY = 8`, a check in `addOwner` that throws when the city is full, and a private `isCityOwnerLimitReached(city)` helper. City matching ignores case, consistent with the existing `resolveCity`/`nextCustomerCode` logic; a `null` city is never treated as full.\n\n**Test change (necessary):**\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.registerOwnersToday` previously put all 19\u201320 daily-limit registrants in **Madison**. That incidentally violated the new per-city cap, causing `createOwnerAllowedWhenBelowDailyLimit` (which expects 201 with 23 Madison owners) to fail \u2014 an unavoidable conflict, since the spec unambiguously requires rejecting a city with \u22658 owners. Because the daily limit counts by registration date and is independent of city, I spread those registrants across distinct cities (`\"DailyCity\" + i`), preserving the test's original intent while removing the incidental collision.\n\nThe full suite (287 tests) is green.",
